<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The window the OS paints from launch until Flutter's first frame.

  A flat fill of @color/lc_canvas — the app's own canvas, light or dark by
  resource qualifier — and nothing else. Nothing else, because frame 0 of
  `StartupIntro` is that same canvas with the mark still at opacity 0: a flat
  fill is the pixel-exact still of the frame it hands over to, and any artwork
  drawn here would *create* the discontinuity rather than cover one.

  This describes this drawable, which is the whole of the launch window only
  below API 31. From Android 12 the platform draws a splash screen of its own
  instead, and it centres `android:windowSplashScreenAnimatedIcon` — defaulting
  to @mipmap/ic_launcher — on the background colour set in `values-v31/`. So on
  a current phone the user does see the launcher icon on the canvas before the
  icon-less first frame. That is the platform's doing and not a contract
  breach: the icon sits on the app's own ground rather than on white, which is
  the whole point of this card. Whether that hand-over reads cleanly is on the
  device-check list.

  `drawable-v21/launch_background.xml` is the same file for API 21 and above,
  which is every device this app runs on (minSdk 24); both are kept pointed at
  the same colour so neither can be the one that is stale.
-->
<layer-list xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <item android:drawable="@color/lc_canvas" />
</layer-list>
